<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.3.5: Using the Factor Theorem</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01623</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.3.5: Using the Factor Theorem</md:title>
    <md:uuid>5a75d066-60bf-48a9-b03d-b7c239a71c3e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
<para id="para-00001">In all the division problems we completed that had a remainder of zero, we say that the divisor was a factor of the dividend.</para>
<para id="para-00002">Whenever a divisor, \(x-c\), divides a polynomial function, \(f(x)\), and results in a remainder of zero, we say \(x-c\) is a factor of \(f(x)\).</para>
<para id="para-00003">This leads us to the Factor Theorem.</para>

<note id="note-00001">
  <para id="para-00004"><emphasis>FACTOR THEOREM</emphasis></para>
  
  <para id="para-00005">For any polynomial function \(f(x)\),</para>
  <list id="list-00001">
    <item>if \(x-c\) is a factor of \(f(x)\), then \(f(c)=0\).</item>
    <item>if \(f(c)=0\), then \(x-c\) is a factor of \(f(x)\).</item>
  </list>
</note>

<para id="para-00006">Use the Factor Theorem to determine whether the binomial is a factor of the given polynomial function. Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00001">
  <problem id="prob-00001">
    <para id="para-00007">Is \((x - 5)\) a factor of \(f(x)=x^3-125\)?</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00001">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00008">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00009">Yes, because \(f(5) = (5)^3 - 125 = 125 - 125 = 0\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00002">
  <problem id="prob-00002">
    <para id="para-00010">Is \((x - 6)\) a factor of \(f(x)=x^3-216\)?</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00002">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00011">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00012">Yes, because \(f(6) = (6)^3 - 216 = 216 - 216 = 0\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00003">
  <problem id="prob-00003">
    <para id="para-00013">Is \((x + 3)\) a factor of \(f(x)=x^3+8x^2+21x+18\)?</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00003">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00014">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00015">Yes, because \(f(-3) = (-3)^3 + 8(-3)^2 + 21(-3) + 18 = -27 + 72 - 63 + 18 = 0\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00004">
  <problem id="prob-00004">
    <para id="para-00016">Is \((x + 4)\) a factor of \(f(x)=x^3+x^2-14x+8\)?
    </para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00004">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00017">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00018">No, because \(f(-4) = (-4)^3 + (-4)^2 - 14(-4) + 8 = -64 + 16 + 56 + 8 = 16\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
</content>
